1
00:00:00,467 --> 00:00:02,200
How many strangers would you need

2
00:00:02,200 --> 00:00:04,567
in a room before a shared birthday

3
00:00:04,567 --> 00:00:06,500
becomes more likely than not?

4
00:00:07,233 --> 00:00:09,067
A hundred? Two hundred?

5
00:00:10,000 --> 00:00:11,300
Try twenty-three.

6
00:00:12,133 --> 00:00:13,433
If that feels wrong,

7
00:00:13,433 --> 00:00:14,967
hold on to that feeling.

8
00:00:15,433 --> 00:00:17,200
It points to the exact mistake

9
00:00:17,200 --> 00:00:18,733
this puzzle exposes.

10
00:00:19,233 --> 00:00:21,067
You may be imagining someone

11
00:00:21,067 --> 00:00:22,500
matching your birthday.

12
00:00:22,900 --> 00:00:24,867
But nobody has to match you.

13
00:00:25,533 --> 00:00:27,267
Any two people can match.

14
00:00:27,733 --> 00:00:29,667
And every person who enters

15
00:00:29,667 --> 00:00:31,000
creates more chances

16
00:00:31,000 --> 00:00:32,167
than the one before.

17
00:00:32,567 --> 00:00:34,033
The second person creates

18
00:00:34,033 --> 00:00:35,533
one possible pair.

19
00:00:35,967 --> 00:00:37,667
The third adds two more.

20
00:00:38,067 --> 00:00:39,667
By the time twenty-three people

21
00:00:39,667 --> 00:00:40,967
are standing here,

22
00:00:40,967 --> 00:00:43,533
there are 253 pairs.

23
00:00:43,933 --> 00:00:46,167
You were looking at a small crowd.

24
00:00:46,167 --> 00:00:47,533
The probability was looking

25
00:00:47,533 --> 00:00:49,067
at a much bigger network.

26
00:00:49,500 --> 00:00:52,100
But counting pairs is only the
clue.

27
00:00:52,100 --> 00:00:53,700
Here is the calculation.

28
00:00:54,167 --> 00:00:55,133
Instead of asking

29
00:00:55,133 --> 00:00:56,633
when birthdays collide,

30
00:00:56,967 --> 00:00:57,867
ask what it takes

31
00:00:57,867 --> 00:01:00,067
for every birthday to be
different.

32
00:01:00,500 --> 00:01:01,900
The first person can have

33
00:01:01,900 --> 00:01:02,933
any birthday.

34
00:01:03,367 --> 00:01:04,600
The second must avoid

35
00:01:04,600 --> 00:01:06,200
one occupied date.

36
00:01:06,700 --> 00:01:11,533
That leaves 364 out of 365 days.

37
00:01:11,967 --> 00:01:14,067
If both birthdays are different,

38
00:01:14,067 --> 00:01:16,167
the third must avoid two dates.

39
00:01:16,633 --> 00:01:19,633
Now 363 days remain.

40
00:01:20,033 --> 00:01:22,133
Keep multiplying those chances

41
00:01:22,133 --> 00:01:23,367
as people enter.

42
00:01:23,900 --> 00:01:25,533
At twenty-three people,

43
00:01:25,533 --> 00:01:27,100
the chance that every birthday

44
00:01:27,100 --> 00:01:30,633
is different is about 49.3%.

45
00:01:31,100 --> 00:01:32,833
So at least one shared birthday

46
00:01:32,833 --> 00:01:33,767
is the rest:

47
00:01:34,100 --> 00:01:36,267
about 50.7%.

48
00:01:37,067 --> 00:01:39,100
For this classic calculation,

49
00:01:39,100 --> 00:01:41,033
assume independent birthdays,

50
00:01:41,300 --> 00:01:44,900
equally likely across 365 days.

51
00:01:44,900 --> 00:01:46,200
Ignore leap day.

52
00:01:46,700 --> 00:01:48,400
That does not promise a match

53
00:01:48,400 --> 00:01:49,900
in this particular room.

54
00:01:50,367 --> 00:01:53,000
It means that across many such
rooms,

55
00:01:53,000 --> 00:01:54,733
just over half would have one.

56
00:01:55,200 --> 00:01:56,600
And with fifty people,

57
00:01:56,600 --> 00:01:59,200
the chance exceeds 97%.

58
00:01:59,700 --> 00:02:01,067
The surprise was never

59
00:02:01,067 --> 00:02:03,133
how many birthdays fit in a year.

60
00:02:03,633 --> 00:02:05,133
It was how many comparisons

61
00:02:05,133 --> 00:02:06,300
fit in a room.

62
00:02:06,733 --> 00:02:08,833
Your intuition counted people.

63
00:02:08,833 --> 00:02:10,967
The probability counted pairs.

64
00:02:11,167 --> 00:02:12,667
Smarter choices.

65
00:02:12,967 --> 00:02:14,400
A better future.

66
00:02:14,700 --> 00:02:16,133
Follow for more.
